# Lines scripts/check-status-words.sh accepts even though its search finds a
# comparison with a status word (Done, Shipped, Closed, Completed, Verified)
# outside the shared readers.
#
# Format, tab-separated, three fields:
#
#   <file>  <match>  <reason>
#
#   file    repository-relative path under skills/ or crates/
#   match   a fixed string (not a regex); the record covers every flagged line
#           of file that contains it
#   reason  why the line is not a milestone status read; required, an empty
#           reason is an error
#
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. The check fails on a record that
# covers no flagged line (stale), on an empty match or reason, and on a file
# outside skills/ and crates/, so a record here stays tied to a line that
# still needs it.
#
# Prefer reading the status through its properties over a record: Rust asks
# crates/shirabe-validate/src/statuses.rs, a coordinate script asks
# lib_status_prop. A record belongs only where the word is not a milestone's
# status (a document's own status, a GitHub state, the text of a message, the
# exact word a writer has just written), or where a v1-v3 read is legacy
# behaviour kept on purpose because reading it through the table would change
# what the tool does; such a reason says so and names the change it avoids.

crates/shirabe-validate/src/checks.rs	!doc.status.trim().eq_ignore_ascii_case("Done")	FC21 on a v4 roadmap reads the roadmap document's own status, not a milestone's; the milestones are then read through statuses::table()
crates/shirabe-validate/src/checks.rs	observed == IssueState::Closed	a GitHub issue's observed state (IssueState::Closed), not a status word
crates/shirabe-validate/src/lifecycle.rs	e.message.contains("expected status 'Done'")	a lifecycle test asserting the text of an L01 message about a BRIEF document's status
crates/shirabe/src/migrate.rs	if doc.status.trim() == "Done" {	migrate refuses a roadmap whose document status is Done; a document status, not a milestone's
crates/shirabe/src/migrate.rs	v.starts_with("verified") => "Verified".to_string()	migrate compares the verdict word (verified) and writes Verified; the old status is read through table.props above it
skills/coordinate/scripts/pick-facts_test.sh	select(.status == "Done" and .done != true and .holding == null)	asserts the decider fixtures hold no row pick-facts.sh would have collapsed; reads fixture data, not a roadmap
skills/coordinate/scripts/reconcile-report.sh	elif .what == "verdict owed" then "verdict owed: the roadmap reads it Completed	compares the change kind (verdict owed); Completed is in the message text
skills/coordinate/scripts/roadmap-status-v4_test.sh	test("verdict is owed.*sets it Completed")	asserts the text of the roadmap edit's message
skills/coordinate/scripts/roadmap-status.sh	in Done*) refuse "$TAG already reads Done on $DEFAULT_BRANCH"	v1-v3 legacy behaviour left as it was on purpose: the verdict writer refuses a second verdict only on a block reading Done*, so a Shipped or Closed milestone can still take one; v4 goes through lib_status_move on the line before
skills/coordinate/scripts/roadmap-status.sh	jq -e '.status == "Done"'	the v1-v3 verdict writer checks the block now carries the Done it just wrote, a read of its own write rather than of a status someone else set
skills/work-on/scripts/run-cascade.sh	if [[ "$line" != "Done" ]]; then	roadmap/v1 legacy behaviour left as it was on purpose: the completion cascade edits only roadmap/v1 feature roadmaps (milestone roadmaps are left alone) and deletes one only when every feature reads exactly Done, so a Shipped or Closed feature keeps the roadmap; reading it through the table would widen that deletion
skills/work-on/scripts/run-cascade_test.sh	[[ "$feature_status" == "**Status:** Done" ]]	asserts the cascade wrote Done into a roadmap/v1 feature roadmap
